  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nThis morning's observations reveal a cold, calm night in the UP \nunder high pressure. At the mid-levels, RAP analysis shows a \nlongwave trough dropping down through the entirety of the Great \nPlains, while ridging spans much of the eastern US. At the surface, \nthe UP sits under a relatively loose pressure gradient on the back \nend of high pressure centered above Georgian Bay. Clear skies over \nthe UP for the first half of the night (now clouding over from the \nsouthwest) have allowed for efficient radiational cooling, as well \nas a unobscured view of tonight's full moon. Winds have been calm \nthus far tonight with evidence of land breezes on Lakes Superior and \nMichigan. Temperatures are dropping steadily into the single digits \nin the interior west and central UP, while lingering in the teens in \nthe east and low 20s near lakeshores.\n\nAs high pressure moves east toward the Canadian Maritimes over the \nnext few days, the area remains under trailing ridging well into \nThursday. Dry conditions and benign winds will play out over the \nnext few days, with tonight the chilliest as a warming trend takes \nhold into the weekend. By Thursday, highs and lows trend anomalously \nwarm (0-10F above), though at this time of year that means highs in \nthe low 40s Thanksgiving Day and Friday, and lows in the high teens \nand 20s. According to GLSEA analysis, the surface of Lake Superior \nis averaging 3-4F above normal at 46F, and Lake Michigan is \naveraging 6F above normal at 52F. This week, lakeshores will see low \ntemperatures over 10F warmer than locations at higher terrain or \nwell inland.\n\nThursday into Friday, a deep low pressure system swings north from \nthe Carolinas toward New York State. While it brings widespread \nmoderate to high precipitation chances downstate, the MQT CWA \ndoesn't see more than 10% chance through Friday as steering flow \ndirects the system eastward. Saturday, a shortwave trough passes \nthrough the area and gives a little vorticity bump, but is likely to \nresult in minimal precipitation (10-20% chance of greater than 0.10\" \nof QPF) given a dry atmosphere with less than 0.5\" of PWAT. Sunday \ninto Monday, an Alberta Clipper passes north of us on its way from \nManitoba to Ontario with PoPs up to 50%. By Monday, 850mb \ntemperatures reach -10C with steady northwesterlies, indicating lake \neffect snow as the precipitation mechanism. QPF remains low, though, \nwith 20-50% chance of QPF greater than 0.10\" for each Sunday and \nMonday.",
